    "safety": {
      "cautions": [
        "Stop if you feel sharp, radiating or nerve-type pain.",
        "Muscle glow colours and activation curves are illustrative, not measured EMG.",
        "Low-back pain: keep the range small and the knees bent, or keep the feet on the floor; stop if twisting provokes pain (clinical opinion).",
        "Osteoporosis or vertebral fracture: avoid repeated loaded spinal flexion and twisting.",
        "Pregnancy and postpartum (diastasis recti): get individual guidance before dynamic trunk-flexion work.",
        "The athlete mesh rests on the mat; the rig collision shapes are thicker than the mesh at the back and buttocks, so floorTolerance records how far those proxy shapes sink below the mat surface."
      ],
      "modify": [
        "Feet on the floor (knee rocks) to make it easier.",
        "Smaller range: knees only part of the way to the floor."
      ],
      "regressions": [
        "Supine knee rocks with the feet on the floor.",
        "Dead bug."
      ],
      "progressions": [
        "Slower lowering with a 2 s pause near the floor.",
        "Straight-leg windshield wipers.",
        "Light medicine ball between the knees."
      ]
    },
    "muscleNotes": {
      "obliques_r": "External and internal obliques lower the legs to the side under control and pull them back to the middle; they are the trunk rotators (the twisting cross curl-up raised external oblique activity over a straight curl-up, Konrad 2001). No study measured this exact drill (clinical opinion for the rest).",
      "obliques_l": "External and internal obliques lower the legs to the side under control and pull them back to the middle; they are the trunk rotators (the twisting cross curl-up raised external oblique activity over a straight curl-up, Konrad 2001). No study measured this exact drill (clinical opinion for the rest).",
      "core": "Rectus abdominis and the deep abdominals hold the pelvis so the low back does not arch as the legs travel (clinical opinion).",
      "hip_flexors_r": "Hold the thighs up at about 90 degrees (tabletop) throughout (clinical opinion).",
      "hip_flexors_l": "Hold the thighs up at about 90 degrees (tabletop) throughout (clinical opinion).",
      "adductors_r": "Keep the knees and feet together (clinical opinion).",
      "adductors_l": "Keep the knees and feet together (clinical opinion).",
      "lats_r": "Press the arms into the mat to keep the shoulders down (clinical opinion).",
      "lats_l": "Press the arms into the mat to keep the shoulders down (clinical opinion).",
      "post_deltoid_r": "Helps press the outstretched arms into the floor for an anchor (clinical opinion).",
      "post_deltoid_l": "Helps press the outstretched arms into the floor for an anchor (clinical opinion)."
    }
  },
